@extends('layouts.app')

@section('title')
  Panel
@endsection

@section('content')
  <h1>{{ $title }}</h1>
  <p>The panel is the admin area of the app: its address, its configuration, its sign-in, and the messages it shows after a change.</p>

  <h2>Address</h2>
  <p>The panel mounts under <code>/admin</code>. The <code>mount</code> method changes the address, with or without a trailing slash:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">use ignite_panel::Ignite;

/// The panel at its own address, instead of the default `/admin`.
pub fn panel() -&gt; Ignite {
    Ignite::new().mount("/backoffice")
}</code></pre>
  <p>The panel's routes are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>GET /admin</code>, the dashboard.</li>
    <li><code>GET /admin/login</code>, <code>POST /admin/login</code>, <code>POST /admin/logout</code> and <code>POST /admin/logout-everywhere</code>, for sign-in, sign-out and ending every session of the user.</li>
    <li><code>GET /admin/&lt;slug&gt;</code>, the list of a resource, with <code>q</code>, <code>sort</code>, <code>dir</code> and <code>page</code> in the query string.</li>
    <li><code>GET /admin/&lt;slug&gt;/new</code> and <code>POST /admin/&lt;slug&gt;</code>, to create a row.</li>
    <li><code>GET /admin/&lt;slug&gt;/&lt;id&gt;/edit</code> and <code>POST /admin/&lt;slug&gt;/&lt;id&gt;</code>, to update a row.</li>
    <li><code>POST /admin/&lt;slug&gt;/&lt;id&gt;/delete</code>, to delete a row.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Configuration</h2>
  <p>These environment variables matter. They can come from the environment or from a <code>.env</code> file in development:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>DATABASE_URL</code>, a <code>mysql://</code> URL. The panel panics at startup if it is missing or the database cannot be reached.</li>
    <li><code>IGNITE_SECRET_KEY</code>, a base64 string of 32 bytes. It signs the sign-in and flash cookies. Create one with <code>openssl rand -base64 32</code>. A release build stops at startup without a valid key. A debug build uses a random key for that run, with a warning, so sign-ins end when the app restarts.</li>
    <li><code>IGNITE_VIEWS_DIR</code>, a folder of the panel's views that replaces the embedded ones, for editing views without a rebuild. It works in debug builds only; a release build ignores it. See <a href="/docs/views">Views</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Sign-in</h2>
  <p>The panel's users live in the <code>users</code> table, which the panel owns: an id, a name, an email address that must be unique, a password hash and a creation time. Passwords are hashed with argon2.</p>
  <p>Sign in at <code>/admin/login</code>. A wrong email or password shows the same message for both, so the page does not reveal which emails are registered.</p>
  <p>A sign-in is a row in the <code>sessions</code> table, and it lasts eight hours. The browser holds only a signed cookie with the row's id, and the panel checks the row on each request. Sign-out deletes the row. The header's <code>Sign out everywhere</code> button, which posts to <code>/admin/logout-everywhere</code>, deletes every session of the signed-in user.</p>
  <p>Every page under the panel needs a session, except sign-in and sign-out. Without one, the request redirects to <code>/admin/login</code>.</p>
  <p>The panel has no sign-up page, no password reset, no remember-me and no roles. To add a user, use the command-line tool in <a href="/docs/install">Installation</a>. It checks the configuration and the email before it asks for the password:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-bash">cargo run --bin ignite -- user create --email you@example.com --name "You"</code></pre>

  <h2>Forgery protection</h2>
  <p>Each browser has a token, held in the signed <code>ignite_csrf</code> cookie and created on the first panel page it renders. Every panel form carries it as a hidden <code>_token</code> field. Sign-in, sign-out, log out everywhere, create, update and delete check the token before they do anything. A missing or wrong token gets status 419 and a Page Expired page. Your app's own routes are checked the same way, as the next section shows.</p>

  <h2>Login throttle</h2>
  <p>Each failed sign-in counts against its email, five per 60 seconds, and against its IP address, twenty per 60 seconds. The counts are kept in the <code>login_throttles</code> table, so they hold across restarts and processes. While either count is at its limit, sign-in is refused with status 429, a <code>Retry-After</code> header with the seconds to wait, and the sign-in page. A successful sign-in clears the email's count. Requests whose address is unknown share one IP count.</p>

  <h2>Your own pages</h2>
  <p>A route of the app under the panel's address can require a sign-in. Take <code>ignite_panel::AdminUser</code> as an argument, and it is a request guard: without a session the request redirects to the sign-in page. <code>user_id()</code> returns the signed-in user's id. This route also takes <code>ignite_panel::PanelStyles</code>, so its view gets the panel's stylesheet. It renders the view in <a href="/docs/views">Views</a>:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">use ignite_panel::{AdminUser, PanelStyles};
use propel_rocket::PropelView;
use serde::Serialize;

#[derive(Serialize)]
pub struct Account {
    pub user_id: i64,
    pub ignite_styles: String,
}

/// An app page under the panel's mount. The `AdminUser` guard redirects to the
/// sign-in page when nobody is signed in.
#[rocket::get("/admin/account")]
pub fn account(session: AdminUser, styles: PanelStyles) -&gt; PropelView {
    PropelView::render(
        "account",
        &amp;Account {
            user_id: session.user_id(),
            ignite_styles: styles.html().to_string(),
        },
    )
}</code></pre>

  <h2>Forms of your own</h2>
  <p>An app route that takes a form checks the panel's token. Its data guard is <code>ignite_panel::CsrfForm</code>, which reads <code>_token</code> from the form, or the <code>X-CSRF-TOKEN</code> header, and answers 419 when it is missing or wrong. The page gets the token from <code>ignite_panel::CsrfToken</code>, and writes it into a hidden field. The guard covers POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE routes.</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">use ignite_panel::{CsrfForm, CsrfToken};
use propel_rocket::PropelView;
use serde::Serialize;

#[derive(Serialize)]
pub struct Checkout {
    pub csrf_token: String,
}

/// The form page. The token comes from the request guard, and the view writes
/// it into a hidden `_token` field.
#[rocket::get("/checkout")]
pub fn checkout(token: CsrfToken) -&gt; PropelView {
    PropelView::render(
        "checkout",
        &amp;Checkout {
            csrf_token: token.value().to_string(),
        },
    )
}

/// The form's POST route. `CsrfForm` checks the token before the handler runs,
/// and answers 419 when it is missing or wrong.
#[rocket::post("/checkout", data = "&lt;form&gt;")]
pub fn place_order(form: CsrfForm) -&gt; String {
    form.get("item").unwrap_or_default().to_string()
}

/// A webhook from another service. It has no form token, so the app exempts
/// `/hooks/*` in the builder.
#[rocket::post("/hooks/payment")]
pub fn payment_hook() -&gt; &amp;'static str {
    "ok"
}</code></pre>
  <p>The form writes the token from its context:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-blade">@@extends('layouts.app')

@@section('title')
  Checkout
@@endsection

@@section('content')
  &lt;form method="post" action="/checkout"&gt;
    &lt;input type="hidden" name="_token" value="@{{ $csrf_token }}"&gt;
    &lt;input type="text" name="item"&gt;
    &lt;button type="submit"&gt;Order&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;/form&gt;
@@endsection</code></pre>
  <p>A route that receives webhooks from another service has no form token. Name its path in the builder with <code>csrf_exempt</code>, as <code>.csrf_exempt(&amp;["/hooks/*"])</code> does here. A path ending in <code>/*</code> covers everything under it. The panel's own routes are always checked.</p>

  <h2>Messages</h2>
  <p>After a change, the panel shows a message on the next page. See <a href="/docs/notifications">Notifications</a>.</p>

@endsection
